---
import BlogPost from '../../components/BlogPost.astro';

const link =
  'font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum';
---

<BlogPost
  title="The Size Chart Is Quietly Disappearing | Attirel Blog"
  description="Zara publishes no brand-level size chart. H&M pulled theirs in North America. Where sizing data actually lives now, and what it means when you shop."
  heading="The Size Chart Is Quietly Disappearing"
  date="2026-08-14"
  readingTime="5 min read"
>
  <p>
    Something we noticed these past few weeks, while checking our size guides against the
    retailers' own published charts. That is routine maintenance for a site like this: pull up
    the brand's chart, compare it to ours, fix what drifted. It stopped being routine when we
    went looking for Zara's chart and found that there isn't one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Not a hard-to-find one. Not an outdated one. Zara, one of the largest clothing retailers in
    the world, does not publish a brand-level size chart at all. <a
      href="https://www.zara.com/ww/en/help-center/MySize"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener"
      class={link}>Its help centre</a
    > says measurements live on each product page: select a size on any garment, and Zara shows
    you the body measurements that size fits, for that garment. There is a "Find your size" tool
    that asks for your measurements and fit preference, again per item. Every "Zara size chart"
    you have ever seen online is a third-party reconstruction, and the ones in circulation
    disagree with each other on both the measurements and the letter-to-EU mapping. Ours did
    too, which is why it is no longer on our <a href="/size-guides/zara/" class={link}
      >Zara guide</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>Once you know to look for it, the pattern shows up elsewhere.</p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">
    H&amp;M has withdrawn its chart in North America
  </h2>
  <p>
    H&amp;M still publishes full brand-level tables, but not everywhere. Its US and Canadian
    sites no longer carry a size guide; both direct shoppers to the measurements on each product
    page. The complete charts survive on H&amp;M's smaller regional sites, which is where we
    sourced the rebuilt tables in our <a href="/size-guides/hm/" class={link}>H&amp;M guide</a>.
    An American shopper who goes looking for H&amp;M's size chart on H&amp;M's American site
    will not find one.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is a softer version of the Zara position. The data still exists and it is still
    H&amp;M's own, but the brand has stopped presenting it as the primary answer in its largest
    market.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">The big publishers are hedging too</h2>
  <p>
    Gap and Nike still publish brand-level charts, and good ones. We verified both these past
    few weeks, down to the odd details: Nike's men's chart lists hip and chest as identical
    values from XS through L, which turns out to be Nike's own published data and not anyone's
    transcription error, and its women's regular and plus charts overlap at the boundary, so a
    46-inch bust is both an XXL and a 0X depending on which table you read. Both are documented
    in our <a href="/size-guides/nike/" class={link}>Nike guide</a>. Gap's charts contain a
    genuine contradiction: its women's and men's bottoms guides translate the same waist
    measurement to different men's sizes, laid out in our <a
      href="/size-guides/gap-size-chart-fit-guide/"
      class={link}>Gap guide</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    But look at where both brands are investing. Gap's product pages carry per-garment fit
    tools. Nike's size-fit pages come with per-category tie-breaker rules and the steady
    accumulation of exceptions: separate Tall columns, separate plus charts, a note that Tall is
    only available for select styles. The brand-level chart is still there, but it is
    increasingly a floor, with the real answer moving to the product page.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Why this is happening</h2>
  <p>
    We can only observe the direction, not the boardroom reasoning, but the shape of it is not
    mysterious. A brand-level chart makes a promise that modern retail has trouble keeping: that
    a Medium is a Medium across thousands of styles, multiple fits, different fabrics, and
    factories on several continents. Every one of the chart quirks we documented in this pass is
    that promise straining. Nike's hip-equals-chest columns, Gap's disagreeing bottoms charts,
    H&amp;M's between-size gaps: these are what it looks like when one table tries to describe a
    catalogue that no longer fits in one table.
  </p>
  <p>
    Per-product measurement is the honest response. Zara's approach concedes that the garment is
    the unit of sizing, not the brand. It is also, not incidentally, the approach that makes
    returns cheaper: a shopper who checked the measurements for the specific garment is less
    likely to send it back.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">What it costs you</h2>
  <p>
    The catch is that per-product sizing moves the work onto the shopper. A brand chart,
    whatever its flaws, let you learn your size once. Per-product measurement asks you to check
    every garment, every time, and it quietly removes the reference point that made comparison
    shopping possible. You cannot ask whether Zara runs small if there is no chart to run small
    against. Sizing becomes something you experience one garment at a time, which is also
    convenient for brands, because it makes inconsistency invisible. A catalogue where every
    item is measured individually can drift however it likes.
  </p>
  <p>The practical advice falls out of that:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
    <li>
      <strong>Measure yourself and keep the numbers current.</strong> Bust, waist, hip, inseam.
      They are the only stable reference left when the charts go away.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>When a brand offers per-garment measurements, use them,</strong> even if you know
      your size in that brand. Your size in the brand is becoming a weaker claim than it used to
      be.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where brand charts still exist, check the date and the source.</strong> Ours carry
      both, and the reason is recent experience: two of the four brands we verified had either
      no chart or a withdrawn one, and the copies circulating on aggregator sites do not know
      that.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">
    Where this leaves size guides, including ours
  </h2>
  <p>
    Honestly: as documentation of a system in retreat. The guides on this site now say plainly
    when a brand publishes nothing (Zara), when a chart comes from a regional site because the
    brand pulled it in the US (H&amp;M), and what the brand's own materials contradict (Gap, <a
      href="/size-guides/skechers-size-chart-fit-guide/"
      class={link}>Skechers</a
    >, Nike). That is more useful than pretending a canonical chart exists where it does not,
    and it is the direction we expect more brands to force on everyone who writes about sizing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The size chart had a good run. What replaces it is more accurate and less convenient, and
    knowing that going in is most of the battle.
  </p>
</BlogPost>
